<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

`sympy_check` is a course-local question type for answers that are symbolic
mathematical expressions.  Instead of comparing the student's text directly
with an answer string, it uses [SymPy](https://www.sympy.org/) to recognize
mathematically equivalent expressions.

For example, if the author supplies `2*x` as the solution, the qtype can
recognize `x+x` and `x*2` as equivalent answers.  It also supports structured
answers such as lists and matrices, indexed variables, and quantum-state ket
notation.

This case study stays at the architectural level.  It shows how a substantial
qtype can keep its CAT-SOOP integration small while placing its domain logic in
an independently testable library.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Source Layout</catsoop-section>

The qtype consists of three source files:

```
__QTYPES__/
└── sympy_check/
    ├── sympy_check.py
    ├── checker.py
    └── test_sympy_check.py
```

Their responsibilities are deliberately separate:

* `sympy_check.py` implements the CAT-SOOP qtype hooks, translates `csq_*`
  settings into checker arguments, and renders feedback.
* `checker.py` parses and compares mathematical expressions without depending
  on a CAT-SOOP page context.
* `test_sympy_check.py` tests the checker library directly and tests the
  adapter with a small fake implementation of the tutor API.

This division is the most important design idea in the example.  CAT-SOOP
integration and symbolic mathematics can evolve and be tested independently.

<catsoop-section>Using the Question Type</catsoop-section>

A basic question needs an answer string and should have a stable name:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question sympy_check&gt;
csq_name = "window_count"
csq_prompt = "A string of length L has how many length-k windows? "
csq_soln = "L - k + 1"
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

This pattern is used in course content to ask students for the number of
length-\(k\) windows in a string of length \(L\).  Answers such as
`1 + L - k` are accepted because they are symbolically equivalent to the
configured solution.

Student input is mathematical source text.  Multiplication should be explicit,
as in `2*x`, and either `^` or `**` may be used for powers.  Common functions
and constants such as `sin`, `cos`, `sqrt`, `exp`, `pi`, `e`, and `i` are
available.

The qtype inherits CAT-SOOP's `smallbox` qtype, so authors get its familiar
single-line input control.  The button normally called **Check** is relabeled
**Check Syntax**.  It parses the current entry and displays the corresponding
typeset expression without grading it.

<catsoop-section>Three Checker Modes</catsoop-section>

The adapter exposes three related comparison modes.

<catsoop-subsection>Formula mode</catsoop-subsection>

The default `sympy_formula_check` mode handles scalar expressions, ordered
lists, and matrices:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question sympy_check&gt;
csq_name = "identity"
csq_soln = "sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2"
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

Nested Python-style lists are treated as matrices, so a solution such as
`[[1,0],[0,1]]` can be compared with an equivalent matrix expression.
Shapes and list order matter.

<catsoop-subsection>Indexed-variable mode</catsoop-subsection>

The array mode treats an indexed expression such as `A[i,j]` as one symbolic
variable rather than as a Python or SymPy indexing operation:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question sympy_check&gt;
csq_name = "indexed_value"
csq_checker = "array"
csq_soln = "A[i,j]"
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

Internally, bracket contents are encoded into legal SymPy symbol names.
`A[i,j]` and `A[j,i]` consequently remain distinct variables.

<catsoop-subsection>Quantum mode</catsoop-subsection>

The quantum checker recognizes binary ket notation and converts states to
vectors for comparison:

<pre><code class="language-python">&lt;question sympy_check&gt;
csq_name = "qubit_state"
csq_checker = "quantum"
csq_soln = "a*|0&gt; + b*|1&gt;"
csq_phase_ambiguity = True
&lt;/question&gt;</code></pre>

With `csq_phase_ambiguity = True`, two states may differ by a single global
phase and still compare as equivalent.  Authors should enable this only when
global phase is irrelevant to the exercise.

<catsoop-section>The Checker Pipeline</catsoop-section>

The reusable `SympyFormulaChecker` follows a staged pipeline:

1. Normalize notation.  This includes HTML entities, `^` powers, matrix
   literals, and any array or ket syntax selected by the checker mode.
2. Parse the submitted and expected strings into SymPy objects.
3. Compare compatible lists and matrices element by element and verify their
   shape.
4. Try direct equality and symbolic simplification.
5. If symbolic comparison is inconclusive, substitute sampled values for free
   symbols and compare numerically within a configured tolerance.

The default sampling range for each free variable is 0.001 to 0.999.  Course
authors can specify a more appropriate domain:

```python
csq_samples = "x,y@-2,-3:2,3#30"
```

The portion before `@` names the variables, the values around `:` give their
lower and upper bounds, and the final number is the trial count.  Explicit
ranges are important when expressions have singularities or variables have a
restricted physical domain.

Numerical comparison can use an absolute tolerance such as `0.002` or a
relative tolerance such as `"3%"`.  Random sampling is a fallback, not a
proof: tests should cover representative domains, and high-consequence
grading rules should use sufficiently strong symbolic conditions and sampling
policies.

<catsoop-section>The CAT-SOOP Adapter</catsoop-section>

The qtype begins by inheriting `smallbox` and loading its sibling checker
module:

```python
_sympy_check_local_import = cs_local_python_import
tutor.qtype_inherit("smallbox")
smallbox, _ = tutor.question("smallbox")

_checker_module = _sympy_check_local_import(
    "checker.py",
    "_catsoop_sympy_check",
)
```

The adapter then extends the inherited defaults with settings including
`csq_checker`, `csq_alt_answers`, `csq_tolerance`, `csq_samples`, and
`csq_phase_ambiguity`.

Its principal qtype hooks are:

* `render_html` delegates to `smallbox["render_html"]`.
* `handle_check` parses the current text and returns a typeset preview.
* `handle_submission` selects a checker, passes it the relevant settings,
  normalizes its return value, and returns CAT-SOOP's `score`/`msg` mapping.
* `answer_display` shows both the source form and a typeset solution.

The public checker functions use CAT-SOOP's conventional interface:

```python
score, message = checker(student_submission, instructor_solution)
```

They return a fractional score and feedback message.  This means the functions
can also be assigned directly to another qtype's `csq_check_function`; they do
not require a course-specific wrapper.

The adapter also preserves CAT-SOOP features around the checker.  It calls an
optional `csq_input_check` before grading, honors an optional
`csq_msg_function`, escapes validation messages before placing them in HTML,
and uses CAT-SOOP's formatted error reporting when a checker raises an
exception.

<catsoop-section>Declarative Settings</catsoop-section>

Content authors normally configure behavior rather than supply new functions:

| Setting | Role |
| --- | --- |
| `csq_checker` | Select formula, array, or quantum mode |
| `csq_alt_answers` | Add other full-credit answer strings |
| `csq_tolerance` | Set absolute or relative numerical tolerance |
| `csq_samples` | Set variables, ranges, and numerical trial count |
| `csq_num_trials` | Change the automatic sampling trial count |
| `csq_only_symbolic` | Require the same parsed SymPy structure |
| `csq_check_none` | Accept `none` or `empty` |
| `csq_phase_ambiguity` | Ignore global phase in quantum mode |
| `csq_extra_symbols` | Add names to the parser's local symbol table |
| `csq_random_seed` | Make numerical sampling reproducible |

For compatibility with older course content, `csq_options` also accepts a
bang-separated string such as:

```python
csq_options = "altanswer='x-1'!tolerance='2%'"
```

The checker parses these course-authored values with `ast.literal_eval`, not
`eval`.  Explicit `csq_*` settings take precedence and are clearer for new
content.

`ast.literal_eval` applies only to the legacy options string.  Mathematical
answers are parsed by SymPy itself.  Because expression parsers process
student-controlled text, deployment owners should keep SymPy current, review
its parsing security model, and use the isolation appropriate for their
checker environment.

<catsoop-section>Testing Strategy</catsoop-section>

Most tests import `checker.py` directly.  They cover:

* equivalent and inequivalent formulas;
* parse errors;
* ordered lists and matrix shapes;
* alternate answers;
* absolute and relative tolerances;
* explicit sampling ranges;
* indexed variables;
* quantum states and global phase; and
* the standard `(score, message)` return convention.

Tests pass a fixed random seed when numerical sampling is involved.  This
makes failures reproducible without changing the behavior used by live
questions.

A smaller group of adapter tests executes `sympy_check.py` with a `FakeTutor`.
The fake supplies only qtype inheritance and the `smallbox` renderer.  These
tests verify that the default checker works without an author-defined
function, declarative mode selection reaches the correct checker, and
rendering is still delegated to `smallbox`.

This is a useful testing pattern for custom qtypes: give domain code ordinary
unit tests, then test the thin CAT-SOOP boundary with a minimal fake context.
Full page tests can be reserved for behavior that genuinely depends on the
loader or default handler.

<catsoop-section>Design Lessons</catsoop-section>

`sympy_check` illustrates several broadly useful practices:

* inherit a built-in qtype when the existing input UI is already suitable;
* put specialized computation in a normal Python module rather than tying it
  to CAT-SOOP globals;
* expose common choices as declarative `csq_*` settings;
* keep legacy configuration at the boundary and normalize it immediately;
* return ordinary CAT-SOOP checker results from reusable checker functions;
* distinguish a non-grading syntax preview from a graded submission; and
* test randomized behavior with an explicit seed.

It also shows why a custom qtype should define a clear policy.  Symbolic
equality, structural equality, approximate numerical equality, indexed
variables, and equivalence up to global phase are different notions of
“correct.”  The qtype makes those choices explicit instead of hiding them in
individual problem code.

For the general interface used by this adapter, see
[Designing Custom Question Types](CURRENT/../qtypes).  For a contrasting
stateful qtype, see the [`self_partner` case study](CURRENT/../self_partner).
